#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134877} :activity/id #uuid "51afd837-2e82-5c9f-a65c-d3df7260a455" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050560 :topic/id #uuid "539a047f-951c-509f-971e-e2ba69953b90" :topic/math-academy-id 2117} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134830 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135109 :tutorial/content "To calculate the circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nFor example, consider the region $D$ below.\n\n![](images/6d/6d7573de141b9a640242644c64fc79a002d93b595fc0d05d1e9ad75317358cb3.png)\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ defined over $D,$ and\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r = 12, \n\\qquad \n\\oint\\limits_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r = -8, \n\\qquad \n\\oint\\limits_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r = -2,\n$$\n\nwhere $C_1$ is oriented counterclockwise, and $C_2,$ and $C_3$ are oriented clockwise. Let's use this information to calculate the value of the circulation $\\Gamma,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{\\partial D}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r.\n$$\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/ab/ab13029d8b9168b384e14d2ae5f10d63c80b3fc8ca46695f59c2405443fa6b14.png)\n\nSince the value of each line integral is given for a positively oriented curve, we can calculate the total circulation over $D$ by summing the circulations over each boundary curve. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ & ={∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =12+(-8)+(-2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "34167048-2745-5bf8-840c-3adfafb899fc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5417 :tutorial/title "Calculating a Circulation Over a Region Containing Holes"} :step/id #uuid "88b043d8-3cd6-5152-ae77-368ed7e7c090" :step/math-academy-id 33792 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134870}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134836 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133886 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133702 :question/id #uuid "d6e8fac3-5dfc-5487-bd81-dc33aeae5f1c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9097"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c67da0d8-518b-5fdb-b6ba-6c1c95c22d9b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050567}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538214} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134100} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369434} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369455} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441617} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538161} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538171} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538207}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying Green's Theorem to Regions With Holes"} :step/id #uuid "66df32bf-a56f-5439-bfeb-551c1de91869" :step/math-academy-id 33916 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134873}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134870 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133899 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133715 :question/id #uuid "18eef985-0fd4-5b08-8072-684ded227ab4" :question/math-academy-id "e-9557"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "56caab23-f54d-58ee-99db-99c651f78e5c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050773}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543841} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369469} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498590} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543836}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Circulation of a Vector Field Over a Region Containing Holes"} :step/id #uuid "41f6c118-0cdf-5a15-8c64-d6983b236e23" :step/math-academy-id 35809 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134872}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134871 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133900 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133716 :question/id #uuid "defe326f-f578-568c-a9b3-3d01f67da058" :question/math-academy-id "e-9558"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdd9b289-4285-5958-a9ef-853882a18d2b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050332}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558972} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134099} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134101} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369518} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441616} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558967}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying Green's Theorem to Regions With Holes: Advanced Cases"} :step/id #uuid "39053d9f-5988-5356-877e-5a36ba9f072c" :step/math-academy-id 35813 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134879}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134872 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135133 :tutorial/content "We now know how to calculate the circulation of a vector field $\\mathbf F$ over a region $D$ that contains holes. \n\nIn previous lessons, we've seen how to use Green's theorem to calculate circulation. Often, Green's theorem allows us to convert a complex line integral over $\\partial D$ to a double integral over $D.$\n\nSo the question is, can we use Green's theorem to calculate the circulation of $\\mathbf F$ over $\\partial D$ when $D$ contains holes?\n\nAs it turns out, the answer is yes. Moreover, we can show that applying Green's theorem to regions containing holes works the same way as with regions that don't have holes. We'll describe this in more detail at the end of the lesson.\n\nWe can summarize Green's theorem to regions containing holes in a single picture, shown below. \n\n![](images/37/379238518375618b14a550ef571564ae4fa74238c9b112e80c559c6e54e74eec.png)\n\nNote that the region $D$ can contain a finite number of holes." :tutorial/id #uuid "50e81026-984c-52b1-8e07-d3af0248f817" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5846 :tutorial/title "Extending Green's Theorem to Regions With Holes"} :step/id #uuid "f5454bd6-7e25-502d-b366-8ec1bf0b61f6" :step/math-academy-id 35820 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134878}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134873 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135134 :tutorial/content "Let's suppose that we want to calculate the double integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_\\limits{D} f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region shown below. \n\n![](images/65/65f622409e3fdd43f56dde79513e8693751465bb131dd828ce9f6cdfaa7d48aa.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the region $D$ can be represented as the difference of sets $D = \\Delta_1\\setminus\\Delta_2,$ where\n\n- $\\Delta_1 = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -3 \\leq x \\leq 4, -2 \\leq y \\leq 3 \\big\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- $\\Delta_2 = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq x \\leq 3, 1 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nIn such cases, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn other words, we can calculate a double integral over a region with a hole as the difference between two integrals." :tutorial/id #uuid "7d5a2750-408d-5d86-9d7b-2c95f46f262b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5847 :tutorial/title "A Note About Double Integrals Over Regions With Holes"} :step/id #uuid "fd423cb3-f8b0-5771-8570-3cd43966acf7" :step/math-academy-id 35821 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134871}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134877 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135138 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of a vector field $\\mathbf F$ over the boundary of the region $D$ shown below. \n\n![](images/35/35a04c813c0b6922c6ee1a71997b9ca0c01a532dde274ca84165cfd97f511201.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is the region between the closed curves $C_1$ and $C_2,$ and has a hole. \n\nWhen dealing with more complex regions, we usually write $\\partial D$ to refer to the boundary of $D.$ So, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\partial D = C_1 \\cup C_2.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe circulation of $\\mathbf F$ over the boundary of $D$ is simply the sum of the circulations over the two bounding curves:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma \n= \\oint\\limits_{\\partial D} \\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d\\mathbf r\n=\n\\oint\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d\\mathbf r\n+\n\\oint\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\nNotice that we have not yet determined the orientations of $C_1$ and $C_2.$ For this, we note the following important points:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve $C_1$ because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse $C_1$ counterclockwise, and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curve $C_2$ because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse $C_2$ clockwise.\n\nTherefore, to apply the above formula for the circulation, we need to choose our orientations as follows:\n\n![](images/54/5496ce5a457601608d5345f4176dc918b12ac090d5f8a77d8a834986352d3064.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c36205a2-23f5-5757-a4ad-9c15c9db94a9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5858 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3005d63f-0b0f-5555-8160-4f0d255657f0" :step/math-academy-id 35870 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134830}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134878 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135139 :tutorial/content "To calculate the circulation of a vector field over a region $D$ containing holes, we add together the circulations over each positively oriented boundary. The outer curves must be oriented counterclockwise for this to work, whereas the inner curves must be oriented clockwise.\n\nTo make sense of why we need to choose our orientations in this way, we turn to Green's theorem. \n\nRecall that Green's theorem tells us that the macroscopic circulation over $\\partial D$ equals the sum of the scalar curls inside $D.$\n\n![](images/37/379238518375618b14a550ef571564ae4fa74238c9b112e80c559c6e54e74eec.png)\n\nNow, if we exaggerate the size of two of the curls inside $D,$ we get the following picture:\n\n![](images/5a/5aa0a1f7a3b021ac85984b8af188a83da5e4f990c4a188676a90ef6d1cf45a61.png)\n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- For the curls' direction to match with the orientation of the *outer* curve at the points where they are in close contact, the outer curve must be oriented *counterclockwise*.\n\n- Similarly, for the curls' direction to match with the orientation of the *inner* curve at the points where they are in close contact, the inner curve must be oriented *clockwise*.\n\nSo, for Green's theorem to give us the correct answer, the inner and outer curves must be oriented clockwise and counterclockwise, respectively." :tutorial/id #uuid "1905ba8e-41ed-5562-9da9-28cae31e9c85" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5859 :tutorial/title "Intuition Behind the Choice of Orentations"} :step/id #uuid "e97aaf9a-9d42-503c-aa26-257a849e5339" :step/math-academy-id 35871 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134836}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134879 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135140 :tutorial/content "When proving Green's theorem, we typically start by assuming that $D$ is a so-called **simple region** (that is, a region that is both type I and type II). Then, Green's theorem is *extended* to include regions that are not simple.\n\nLet's suppose that we've proved that Green's theorem is true for type I and type II regions. How can we use this to show that Green's theorem is also true for regions containing holes?\n\nTo illustrate how this is done, let's consider the vector field $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j,$ and the line integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{\\partial D} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $D$ is the annular region below.\n\n![](images/57/57c22c3d7a92ce4a1a1a798f7e6a861ded2e9a6be4d8d98c36869fe29e2783d0.png)\n\nNote that $\\partial D= C_1\\cup C_2$ is positively oriented. \n\nNow, imagine splitting $D$ into two regions $D_1$ and $D_2$ by means of *cuts* along the segments $[-4, -2]$ and $[2,4]$ on the $x$-axis, as shown below \n\n![](images/e7/e7f0d41c3fb909073cdaf4ea23f4ab617032e0d24bdc8d9b9e3724e7d56abe3b.png)\n\nNotice that $D_1$ and $D_2$ are positively oriented type I regions. In addition, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1} & ={C}_{3}\\cup {C}_{8} \\\\ {C}_{2} & ={C}_{5}\\cup {C}_{10} \\\\ ∂{D}_{1} & ={C}_{3}\\cup {C}_{4}\\cup {C}_{5}\\cup {C}_{6}, \\\\ ∂{D}_{2} & ={C}_{8}\\cup (-{C}_{6})\\cup {C}_{10}\\cup (-{C}_{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the circulation of $\\mathbf F$ over $\\partial D$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup \\,{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{8}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{5}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{10}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & +{{{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{4}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{6}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{-{C}_{6}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r}_{⏟}}_{0} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{5}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{6}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & +{\\int }_{{C}_{8}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{6}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{10}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{-{C}_{4}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={∮}_{∂{D}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{∂{D}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={∮}_{∂{D}_{1}}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y+{∮}_{∂{D}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo summarize,\n\n$$\n\\oint \\limits_{\\partial D}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = \\iint_\\limits{D}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is just Green's theorem for the region $D.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6700e03f-e7d7-5f2c-88cb-17e9c69b7ed5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5860 :tutorial/title "An Illustration of How To Extend Green’s Theorem to Regions Containing Holes"} :step/id #uuid "93e39171-b50b-514c-8900-4f0d3669cc6b" :step/math-academy-id 35872}] :activity/title "Extending Green's Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133998}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441620} :activity/id #uuid "12c751f0-6856-54db-a651-f054b3719043" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050560 :topic/id #uuid "539a047f-951c-509f-971e-e2ba69953b90" :topic/math-academy-id 2117} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441619 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441617 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68a479bb-7541-5424-a117-bc163a220ec5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441596} {:answer/id #uuid "8eb606cd-8859-52ad-923b-4174c43b9bc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441599} {:answer/id #uuid "bab36efc-1119-58ba-bc4b-a7d41daed2a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441602} {:answer/id #uuid "d102eb68-b278-588e-9ae4-718a08bc02d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441604} {:answer/id #uuid "fbd69b33-186c-5c7d-b495-4a5c8ed20698" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441609}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441604} :answer-field/id #uuid "25ffc2c7-e514-54e6-8c7b-600c1de04088" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "93b19a79-b263-5685-8630-5024a89d98bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-126893" :question/problem "![](images/df/df52e2add706a17bf93236cdaa04582932b6c799d4a88a3e194a3a3788c816ff.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}3y\\,dx+7x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3y$ and $Q(x,y)=7x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}dx+x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(7-3)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\,\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 8\\pi  \\\\  & =32\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "01e542b7-96ca-5f0d-8fd7-24a75625fa09" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441623}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441620 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134098 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc41a40b-917a-57ad-93c5-f9db0205c2ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130746} {:answer/id #uuid "34e14694-9434-5ad9-aa2d-2969b9ae1cc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130747} {:answer/id #uuid "4b2ce2f4-0374-5148-947b-96f3d8b6b4ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130748} {:answer/id #uuid "5d7d99a7-294b-5cf9-90af-ed4075071bb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130749} {:answer/id #uuid "323a1bfd-c415-5a52-8f82-c95d150e0d2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441592}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130747} :answer-field/id #uuid "59065be7-12d4-5585-856f-70e63556d4de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "aa43bdf8-83a8-50c7-a713-6a675dec7cf1" :question/math-academy-id "q-126558" :question/problem "![](images/72/7264984a1c19c247c030feae04615bb0c46205ca70475db1240db964ad6007b2.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=10,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-4,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/0d/0de833fcc39b6295418c1558cf1187774db7b4ada1d697f2dcac8c35858563df.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =10-4-2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "39c00f45-8518-5817-af27-2a3b132c427a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441622}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441621 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441616 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12bc592f-463c-5b10-94de-5b44d176d00d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{89}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441590} {:answer/id #uuid "1e63af1d-9e8f-51ce-b89b-69668a98103f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441591} {:answer/id #uuid "6f81656c-ec2b-52fd-99b0-096af9bbcd54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441597} {:answer/id #uuid "e220a614-c017-51de-896b-5544add5e4e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441606} {:answer/id #uuid "fbafbae6-9a9a-5e89-b7aa-04db6e10b82c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{52}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441608}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441608} :answer-field/id #uuid "25134893-42bb-5ae1-bdf7-77b092bdf96e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441611}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "311f57fc-3b6c-595d-a8b9-91e8d4d5d656" :question/math-academy-id "q-126947" :question/problem "![](images/82/823ab320e0f1426b846c7bf561b977ac6458c80d235aabdc5647a7db07c4cb74.png)\n\nCalculate \n${∮}_{∂D}(2x{y}^{3}+{x}^{5})\\,dx+(5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,dy,$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2x{y}^{3}+{x}^{5}$ and $Q(x,y)=5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6x{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=10x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}(2x{y}^{3}+{x}^{5})\\,dx+(5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}10x{y}^{2}-6x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as ${Δ}_{1}∖{Δ}_{2},$ where\n\n- ${Δ}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 2,-2\\le y\\le 1\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- ${Δ}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,-1\\le y\\le 0\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}4x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}4x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-2}^{1}-2{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =(8-2)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}-(-\\frac{8}{3}))-(2-0)\\cdot (0-(-\\frac{1}{3})) \\\\  & =6\\cdot 3-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =18-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{52}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "58400d8d-2398-5d06-be9d-44c7cb7e18b1"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441622 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441618 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4b95dc2-7fee-5f7c-b4ec-8675b76cbf4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441601} {:answer/id #uuid "c1ef271c-da7e-55bf-8af4-8d0b76cc331e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441603} {:answer/id #uuid "d98c6427-b31b-5669-b745-810c5329aad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441605} {:answer/id #uuid "f4bbcaca-d001-596d-9fe9-529dd184cce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441607} {:answer/id #uuid "fedc8124-d82e-59e9-81de-9b44a8c6f376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441610}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441610} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c819006-1c8a-567c-839e-c05698400658" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d4082a24-9ee0-5ed5-bf60-7857736d7c8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-126871" :question/problem "![](images/69/69b42001c8ac5e95e18d89785e73fbba3e78554f9146e59bb50dd618b105f10c.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}4y\\,dx+5x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=4y$ and $Q(x,y)=5x.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=4,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}4y\\,dx+5x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5-4)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 4}{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{2} \\\\  & =8-2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6ae2af08-c3b9-5a97-9f39-1c8f6e552dc4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441619}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441623 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441615 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bc93cb5-dc56-5ce8-9e29-5d04cbab18f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441593} {:answer/id #uuid "590d73f3-e82d-557d-94b6-037ec0668422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,880" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441594} {:answer/id #uuid "63245646-a801-5cdf-a8ba-6eca492c0f0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441595} {:answer/id #uuid "753d4b97-5af5-5ac8-b2eb-b98600ccb71a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1\\,440" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441598} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e4fff1-a46e-50d1-90e7-531220bb1a1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441600}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441595} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7e8d331-dfbd-5435-859b-6678685b6ee8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0e02e690-e43f-5ecb-8751-7e2242b3bc65" :question/math-academy-id "q-126936" :question/problem "![](images/23/23d35daa15e11899592683f2f3215506cc0337358be843780dbf1190c0e38e2c.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}({x}^{2}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}+{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x,y)=({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}({x}^{2}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,4\\le r\\le 8,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{4}^{8}3\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{4}^{8}3\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{4}^{8}3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{4}^{8}3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{4}^{8}3{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{3{r}^{4}}{4}\\,{|}_{4}^{8} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))\\cdot (\\frac{3(8)^{4}}{4}-\\frac{3(4)^{4}}{4}) \\\\  & =0\\cdot (\\frac{3(8)^{4}}{4}-\\frac{3(4)^{4}}{4}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "74228140-f4ff-5d5d-82e9-aeb425e5acdf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441621}}] :activity/title "Extending Green's Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441589}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498629} :activity/id #uuid "b8ec023d-8425-5a86-8f57-a7a99c98e85e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050560 :topic/id #uuid "539a047f-951c-509f-971e-e2ba69953b90" :topic/math-academy-id 2117} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498611 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369434 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fe866fa-6339-5f10-8ce3-3d5938c3785e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369436} {:answer/id #uuid "0aafa5fa-6806-5c1a-bd6e-a8f0ec680d0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369437} {:answer/id #uuid "b169f03c-00d2-5df3-887b-9ea86b4a1f34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369438} {:answer/id #uuid "2e03cd88-a4dd-5d49-b397-096aeb5b4219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369439} {:answer/id #uuid "8e91bdb9-02ae-5c29-aa67-a875b2bc2112" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369440}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369439} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2ee2d3e-bbe0-5737-8885-1738f355a09b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "32a18b85-d978-5b18-8fff-914bdccf82dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-126853" :question/problem "![](images/39/393bed476f2e8d84fc7e5dc721d77749f7f259834e03a94a48a08533ae9befa6.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}2y\\,dx+4x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2y$ and $Q(x,y)=4x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}2y\\,dx+4x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4-2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (6\\cdot 4-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (24-4) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 20 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1b31cf93-d5e4-581a-abf0-1f4ce4b0318a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498614}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498614 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369462 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d7cf9a0-e8a9-528c-9faf-b2259025de3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369464} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1af42a-4bf9-5730-a278-44c2bcb271c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369465} {:answer/id #uuid "26f52db4-19ad-52bc-bdea-a43ddccabe76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369466} {:answer/id #uuid "74b55cd4-e132-5c25-af68-783e5644f0be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369467} {:answer/id #uuid "5901a191-1792-51d7-92cf-8ae7fe658d89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369468}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369468} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0c368b0-4e71-50a8-b81a-cf6d68ab083b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ca63e980-b1ab-5e66-b1ce-ccb3144f7558" :question/math-academy-id "q-126862" :question/problem "![](images/49/49b14da54c952d0585f7aa894d618838ddc91beb65216e0c18c014ba5ad268d7.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=9,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-4,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-6,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/a8/a8e93055fb9a460ab7757475c9c381c191204eae72231921da870b8d727bf988.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =9+(-4)+(-6) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2056fd1b-d5a8-5347-8cd3-9a0ecc4618d9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498640}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498629 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134097 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "074f2b27-d82e-5431-893f-5cb420b7db07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130739} {:answer/id #uuid "4fb000df-3f30-5d92-833d-b570ce4d2fc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130740} {:answer/id #uuid "67a5cc30-ac62-56fb-b984-6bf8c55a68e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130741} {:answer/id #uuid "6d1f5867-3adf-5ebd-8085-208cd0303ff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130742} {:answer/id #uuid "6da1aafe-3858-50f0-8369-80cd33a8831e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130743}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130739} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d6f2a4f-8215-5cfe-aa3b-409cfed8d343" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5d511ff2-5d88-51e6-9da4-d8015a261af1" :question/math-academy-id "q-126515" :question/problem "![](images/32/320a45751b2e718c66bb721cfcd78a2d206f08a9998fbbffddb1581ee995cf34.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}x\\,dy-y\\,dx,$ where $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}x\\,dy-y\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-(-1))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =2\\cdot ({4}^{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (16-4) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6fb02394-525d-5f30-9775-578f4448db13" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498650}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498640 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498490 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb8d995d-0d7c-54d6-8c3f-aa2cc0e562a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498216} {:answer/id #uuid "ce93c3a1-776f-5631-aafd-89bd7f33c5b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "52\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498268} {:answer/id #uuid "cfe50e10-ec80-526e-8c44-4ebed28fb715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "45\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498270} {:answer/id #uuid "e1f112d4-518e-59d2-a8ae-9b7896ed80c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498304} {:answer/id #uuid "e6e01110-89b2-522a-b30e-7b877b8b16ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "54\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498308}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498270} :answer-field/id #uuid "30362ef7-7428-53da-a7f2-7fe7cc3d3d5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "09c35a82-4469-5857-aed4-331c374b8ab3" :question/math-academy-id "q-126895" :question/problem "![](images/0d/0db447407835f74476edd20ed5327ff3cc570905f744bbc6c29a66926913ecb5.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}(1-4y)\\,dx+(2+5x)\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=1-4y$ and $Q(x,y)=2+5x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-4,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}dx+x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5-(-4))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}9\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =9\\,\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =9\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}) \\\\  & =9\\cdot 5\\pi  \\\\  & =45\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "96ebff15-a11c-5705-a99b-83a08193b653"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498650 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498590 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a658638-403e-5ab9-a8f1-33237b22ab86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497887} {:answer/id #uuid "4d5743a8-8fd6-5394-b293-eb9fd93fef49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497979} {:answer/id #uuid "8e9eb833-f8ff-5a68-9ca4-2b64e1730afb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498127} {:answer/id #uuid "9d75ef58-226e-55fd-b151-11b12d2230bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498153} {:answer/id #uuid "f496543e-10b7-5658-8197-b5afed861eb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498334}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497887} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7d01b58-0857-54fb-bdf6-61d33e711eeb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e2260d96-b984-5295-801c-28a9593008a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-126851" :question/problem "![](images/e9/e9892ef4efa3481ad02f7b8e38f1bae1876b196627204521d011656010297281.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=16,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=6,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=4,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/b2/b20b2b911c2a417c26fa951d9b28efa296c265df25e9d06b635ef4846267b93b.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =16+6+4 \\\\  & =26.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c3cbee15-50e1-5853-bd12-2ab6e6059abb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498611}}] :activity/title "Extending Green's Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497793}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519648} :activity/id #uuid "b43c564a-026d-55f8-9ded-681ee43fa117" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050560 :topic/id #uuid "539a047f-951c-509f-971e-e2ba69953b90" :topic/math-academy-id 2117} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519610 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369469 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0b304ac-2b36-5437-b9d8-5fcf23fa4d1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369471} {:answer/id #uuid "a879fd91-6ea0-5ab3-ad43-e4706f2d1bc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369472} {:answer/id #uuid "b0f30d72-4cc9-59bd-b370-c87bbec40944" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369473} {:answer/id #uuid "a8834855-579a-54da-8ae2-aa6847585195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369474} {:answer/id #uuid "253604d5-d42a-5d84-ab1b-eb721ad3403c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369475}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369474} :answer-field/id #uuid "671458dc-075f-5c3e-9fa3-8839124a034e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369470}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9f6dbe2c-628b-581d-a31e-93679dd1a545" :question/math-academy-id "q-126863" :question/problem "![](images/b3/b3562daf036c74c1472931a626d41f28de81e723377597700342823d7f2d1ac2.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=1,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=7,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-4,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/52/527eb8572b90c3d2a4449b69015a9b94f4d121b094be6629e1b92c45f2ff0eb0.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =1+7+(-4) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "02b0f97c-a1c4-5de3-9e6c-ca0b67ea4390" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519667}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519631 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369455 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74b43056-e8dd-536d-b56a-12ac2800c12b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "364" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369457} {:answer/id #uuid "ca09836f-3c6e-5b5f-841d-68ede18363c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "215" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369458} {:answer/id #uuid "ac3caf47-5329-54b1-8c87-bcf0da3ef96b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "424" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369459} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b5b1dc-7bc7-5bb9-8b23-063bc496f471" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "342" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369460} {:answer/id #uuid "1d2e77d0-1779-5c5c-9e64-d3cabd705415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "124" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369461}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369457} :answer-field/id #uuid "175c17d1-364f-5ab7-a6b1-2ce4de237b97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "017f8f34-6e37-562a-87cf-df5a06a7e434" :question/math-academy-id "q-126860" :question/problem "![](images/b5/b5a42039e2501a3909062fd6ed92e0879df03f58fa85a62b5e738e8abc78b903.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}8x\\,dy-5y\\,dx,$ where $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-5y$ and $Q(x,y)=8x.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-5,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}8x\\,dy-5y\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(8-(-5))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}13\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =13{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =13\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =13\\cdot ({8}^{2}-{6}^{2}) \\\\  & =13\\cdot (64-36) \\\\  & =13\\cdot 28 \\\\  & =364.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "48cb7736-a399-57ef-8551-b03d268a4144" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519610}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519647 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441618 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4b95dc2-7fee-5f7c-b4ec-8675b76cbf4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441601} {:answer/id #uuid "c1ef271c-da7e-55bf-8af4-8d0b76cc331e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441603} {:answer/id #uuid "d98c6427-b31b-5669-b745-810c5329aad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441605} {:answer/id #uuid "f4bbcaca-d001-596d-9fe9-529dd184cce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441607} {:answer/id #uuid "fedc8124-d82e-59e9-81de-9b44a8c6f376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441610}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441610} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c819006-1c8a-567c-839e-c05698400658" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d4082a24-9ee0-5ed5-bf60-7857736d7c8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-126871" :question/problem "![](images/69/69b42001c8ac5e95e18d89785e73fbba3e78554f9146e59bb50dd618b105f10c.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}4y\\,dx+5x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=4y$ and $Q(x,y)=5x.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=4,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}4y\\,dx+5x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5-4)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 4}{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{2} \\\\  & =8-2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7855bb63-c81b-53d8-a3eb-d50bbb715b43"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519648 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431351 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ca980d7-830a-5712-906f-d7004a38953f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431285} {:answer/id #uuid "2da41db7-6d0c-5183-9ea5-02fe84e79cce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431292} {:answer/id #uuid "3b720f7a-85ac-5b6e-aa55-b3fe1bb64322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431298} {:answer/id #uuid "750e0059-fe60-559a-b87a-afcd74ddf718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431312} {:answer/id #uuid "8173bde3-749c-53ab-95a4-d623b09672f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431313}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431285} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6feb552-f724-5d46-86df-32005b0ffcc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5c72e07b-dd70-5ff7-8f10-0939e87dedc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-126855" :question/problem "![](images/f2/f26ee97bb47430c6a5ed314af007f49415295f682eeb474af33217ef58999758.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=4,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=9,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-5,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/7b/7b68ab924946d0509b1666b5c0795a7f8987c32aef88ca3ab72746663e9428a8.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =4+9+(-5) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8b29823e-1d02-5228-87de-cfdd6eddac82" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519631}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519667 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519559 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0662a46f-bec7-54ca-b240-f60d5d5f1afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518998} {:answer/id #uuid "0e19b2e0-2b38-57c0-b50d-3e99a4fb345c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519013} {:answer/id #uuid "12efe453-d9bc-5e34-bdeb-04e1a1d22aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519022} {:answer/id #uuid "a0fe6c9c-6868-551d-8a19-e70fb67ab8d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519256} {:answer/id #uuid "d13ea829-20bc-5000-ad9a-b878fad5ad6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519334}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518998} :answer-field/id #uuid "69041780-f401-5aab-a5e0-f58cc39dca02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "881df761-fca2-5afa-aea8-d6a6e63a7521" :question/math-academy-id "q-126866" :question/problem "![](images/0f/0f1dd3c66e3bf7d25f286720b360d6af1d6361804ab3ff52835d94f78e5084ee.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}-3y\\,dx+2x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-3y$ and $Q(x,y)=2x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-3y\\,dx+2x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2-(-3))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =5\\cdot ({4}^{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{2}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (16-2) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 14 \\\\  & =70.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "dcca2676-1c39-51b2-9647-11d87db779a0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519647}}] :activity/title "Extending Green's Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518984}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572240} :activity/id #uuid "c82b05d5-a65f-5e94-82c6-10840d3be8cb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050560 :topic/id #uuid "539a047f-951c-509f-971e-e2ba69953b90" :topic/math-academy-id 2117} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572206 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498590 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a658638-403e-5ab9-a8f1-33237b22ab86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497887} {:answer/id #uuid "4d5743a8-8fd6-5394-b293-eb9fd93fef49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497979} {:answer/id #uuid "8e9eb833-f8ff-5a68-9ca4-2b64e1730afb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498127} {:answer/id #uuid "9d75ef58-226e-55fd-b151-11b12d2230bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498153} {:answer/id #uuid "f496543e-10b7-5658-8197-b5afed861eb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498334}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497887} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7d01b58-0857-54fb-bdf6-61d33e711eeb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e2260d96-b984-5295-801c-28a9593008a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-126851" :question/problem "![](images/e9/e9892ef4efa3481ad02f7b8e38f1bae1876b196627204521d011656010297281.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=16,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=6,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=4,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/b2/b20b2b911c2a417c26fa951d9b28efa296c265df25e9d06b635ef4846267b93b.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =16+6+4 \\\\  & =26.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "11e0ee9b-ca04-51fe-ad89-81bb2c975030" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572251}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572240 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134098 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc41a40b-917a-57ad-93c5-f9db0205c2ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130746} {:answer/id #uuid "34e14694-9434-5ad9-aa2d-2969b9ae1cc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130747} {:answer/id #uuid "4b2ce2f4-0374-5148-947b-96f3d8b6b4ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130748} {:answer/id #uuid "5d7d99a7-294b-5cf9-90af-ed4075071bb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130749} {:answer/id #uuid "323a1bfd-c415-5a52-8f82-c95d150e0d2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441592}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130747} :answer-field/id #uuid "59065be7-12d4-5585-856f-70e63556d4de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "aa43bdf8-83a8-50c7-a713-6a675dec7cf1" :question/math-academy-id "q-126558" :question/problem "![](images/72/7264984a1c19c247c030feae04615bb0c46205ca70475db1240db964ad6007b2.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=10,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-4,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/0d/0de833fcc39b6295418c1558cf1187774db7b4ada1d697f2dcac8c35858563df.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =10-4-2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "66d58fbe-8c45-5ed1-bb3f-c9af86e61ea6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572206}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572251 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431357 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d8f90a5-b39b-592d-a1d9-ab32a0cec7c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431287} {:answer/id #uuid "462f4f66-48fa-5a1b-a234-7ec355e80ccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431303} {:answer/id #uuid "61820d09-1fb4-5a1a-a196-e88b05cc9b4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431309} {:answer/id #uuid "62afb4f8-e3ba-552d-b94f-0f8ee6e59d30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431310} {:answer/id #uuid "b48bbdd2-bf0b-5780-9c2e-2d82fda1acee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431326}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431303} :answer-field/id #uuid "23144688-cc3e-5691-a60e-f350dd3406bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fa88e59c-b562-550a-9e35-b904fd919d3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-126861" :question/problem "![](images/ef/efc1df0808af7ab4dacfb3031fdde2132bceaf0e89f88788110d7b5f5ed688bf.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=7,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-3,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/f7/f7869b25194e311c08df6c5b647fe9c53f7064c94eaa5d60fa1fa2551a6b58a1.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =7+(-3)+2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "95601daf-6a6b-5e4b-81e5-5408489da6fc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572254}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572254 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369462 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d7cf9a0-e8a9-528c-9faf-b2259025de3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369464} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1af42a-4bf9-5730-a278-44c2bcb271c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369465} {:answer/id #uuid "26f52db4-19ad-52bc-bdea-a43ddccabe76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369466} {:answer/id #uuid "74b55cd4-e132-5c25-af68-783e5644f0be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369467} {:answer/id #uuid "5901a191-1792-51d7-92cf-8ae7fe658d89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369468}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369468} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0c368b0-4e71-50a8-b81a-cf6d68ab083b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ca63e980-b1ab-5e66-b1ce-ccb3144f7558" :question/math-academy-id "q-126862" :question/problem "![](images/49/49b14da54c952d0585f7aa894d618838ddc91beb65216e0c18c014ba5ad268d7.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=9,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-4,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-6,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/a8/a8e93055fb9a460ab7757475c9c381c191204eae72231921da870b8d727bf988.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =9+(-4)+(-6) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9da1b9ea-e2e6-5ce9-91d2-2391626e0f0f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572268}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572268 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431354 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0918efff-3716-5e79-bc16-ce01b647dee2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431283} {:answer/id #uuid "0a4ab89c-0bd1-5d9d-bbfe-405c2f326073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431284} {:answer/id #uuid "b158c6b6-52a5-5218-9685-8e39e2b380c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431323} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f98841-106f-5d7f-bf0f-b27f11e0da85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431324} {:answer/id #uuid "fe684fde-4550-539f-a64b-9a955faeea4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431337}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431324} :answer-field/id #uuid "1eb80ec8-d8a5-5f3b-808d-db46b94db516" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a2fd9b28-2de8-59c7-8e8c-46e14a79f680" :question/math-academy-id "q-126880" :question/problem "![](images/65/65047f9bf4f035000175fc55ee3ba6eeb4aa7ec39674a1984b95ba98a3f754f6.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}8y\\,dx+13x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=8y$ and $Q(x,y)=13x.$ So,  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=8,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=13.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}8y\\,dx+13x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(13-8)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5\\,{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (\\frac{6\\cdot 6}{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (18-4) \\\\  & =70.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c5758703-47d4-51a6-aab1-e80954e21045"}] :activity/title "Extending Green's Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571535}]}
